---
import CodeBlock from '../CodeBlock.astro';
import StepCard from '../StepCard.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="s5">
  <div class="num">05</div>
  <h2>Build your website</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    One page, your photos, your words. Leave the clever bits out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start here once you have <a href="#s3">set up Claude Code and CLAUDE.md</a>.
    Use the request below; the optional prompt-writing exercise is not required.
  </p>
  <p>
    HTML describes a page's content. CSS controls its appearance. Claude will
    write them in <code class="i">index.html</code> and <code class="i"
      >style.css</code
    >. You will open the first file in your browser; it will load the second.
    The request excludes scripts, extra instructions a page can run. This page
    only needs text, pictures and styling.
  </p>
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="1" title={'Prepare your material'}>
      <p>
        Inside <code class="i">my-site</code>, make a folder called
        <code class="i">photos</code>. Put a few JPEG or PNG copies in it, not
        your only originals or camera raw files. Photos can contain location
        information and other hidden details; choose copies you are comfortable
        sharing, not just pictures that look harmless.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Claude Code, start your message with <code class="i">/plan</code>
        and ask it to create <code class="i">bio.txt</code>, a plain text file,
        from a paragraph you supply. Include your display name and the email
        address you want visitors to use. Review the plan and approve only
        creating that file, using Auto or manual approval. This avoids
        accidentally saving a formatted document that merely has a .txt name.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
    <StepCard number="2" title={'Send the website request'}>
      <p>
        Stay in Claude Code. Paste the request below, including its
        <code class="i">/plan</code> prefix, and keep its limits and checks if you
        adapt it. The plan should use your existing material and create the two website
        files. It should not need another account, software installation or upload.
        Ask it to remove those steps if it proposes them, then approve the plan using
        Auto if available or manual approval.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Website request: paste into Claude Code'}
    code={'/plan Goal: a one-page personal website for me, a maker and photographer.\n\nContext: I am not a programmer. This project contains CLAUDE.md, a photos folder with images and bio.txt with my display name, bio and public contact email. Ask for any missing details instead of inventing them.\n\nConstraints: create one index.html and one style.css using plain HTML and CSS. No extra software, scripts, sign-ups, tracking or externally loaded files. Do not modify my photos or bio.txt. Do not upload or publish anything. Use a dark background, large photos and readable text. Before finishing, check that every photo is referenced, file paths and links are correct, and the layout fits narrow screens using the tools already available. If you cannot perform a check, stop trying that check, say what remains unverified, and tell me how to check it. Do not install tools to complete checks.\n\nWhat done looks like: I open index.html directly in my browser and see my name, my bio, all my photos in a grid and an email link using the supplied address. Give each portfolio image concise, accurate alternative text in its HTML alt attribute. If you cannot inspect an image, ask me for its description rather than guessing.\n\nOutput format: show me a short plan first and wait for my ok before changing files. When finished, list the files changed, list each photo filename with the image description you wrote, and tell me how to open the page.\n\nExample: a simple photographer portfolio, mostly images with very little text.'}
    copy
  />
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Expected files after the build'}
    code={'my-site/\n├── CLAUDE.md\n├── bio.txt\n├── index.html\n├── style.css\n└── photos/'}
  />
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="3" title={'Check the actual page'}>
      <p>
        In Finder or File Explorer, open <code class="i">my-site</code> and double-click
        <code class="i">index.html</code>. If it opens as text, right-click it
        and use Open With to choose your browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read your name and bio. Count the photos. Make the browser window narrow
        and check that nothing runs off the side or becomes hard to read. Click
        the email link: it should address a new message to you, not send one. If
        no email app is set up, ask Claude to tell you which email address the
        link uses, without changing files.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read the alternative text (image descriptions in HTML alt attributes)
        Claude lists alongside each photo filename. Check that they describe the
        actual photos accurately. These descriptions help people who cannot see
        the images; looking at the page alone will not check them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Claude's report is not proof that the page looks right. It may not have
        browser access; your visual check still matters. If the page is blank or
        photos are missing, confirm you opened this project's
        <code class="i">index.html</code>. Do not install extra software just to
        fix this simple page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Note any problems. Before asking Claude to change the page, make the
        backup in the next step.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
    <StepCard number="4" title={'Back up, then correct anything wrong'}>
      <p>
        Before asking for revisions, wait until Claude finishes. In Finder or
        File Explorer, copy <code class="i">my-site</code> beside itself and name
        the copy <code class="i">my-site-backup</code>. It must be outside
        <code class="i">my-site</code>, never inside any agent's working
        project. Do not open the backup, or the parent folder containing both
        copies, in an agent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Describe one problem in the same Claude Code conversation, starting with <code
          class="i">/plan</code
        >: "/plan The photos are too small when the window is narrow." Review
        the proposed correction before approving it. After the change, refresh
        with Cmd+R on Mac or Ctrl+R on Windows and check again.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once it is right, type <code class="i">/exit</code> in Claude Code and press
        Enter. Make a new backup beside <code class="i">my-site</code> called <code
          class="i">my-site-checked</code
        >. Leave that backup untouched.
      </p>
      <p>
        To recover later, close the agents and keep the damaged folder for now.
        In Finder or File Explorer, make a fresh working copy of your last good
        backup beside it, give the copy a new name such as
        <code class="i">my-site-recovered</code>, and continue in that copy.
        Leave the backup untouched. Use <code class="i">cd</code> to enter the new
        working folder before starting Claude Code in Plan mode again.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Beside, not inside'}
    code={'Chosen location/\n├── my-site/          ← working project\n└── my-site-checked/  ← untouched backup'}
  />
  <p>
    Your checked page works on your computer, not on the public web. The first
    build is complete: you can stop here and keep the <a href="#s7"
      >cheat sheet</a
    >
    for next time. Publishing is optional; the
    <a href="/publish/index.html">publishing guide</a> explains how to share it using
    free hosting.
  </p>
</div>
